RECON: GraphQL endpoint accepts batched queries via aliases. Each request can contain up to 100 aliased operations. The rate limiter counts HTTP requests, not GraphQL operations. This means an attacker can send 100 login attempts per HTTP request, effectively multiplying the brute-force rate by 100x.

HYPOTHESIS: rate limiting bypass via GraphQL batching. The WAF/rate limiter sees 1 HTTP request, but the resolver processes 100 login operations. This is a form of OWASP API #4 (Unrestricted Resource Consumption).

TEST: minimal probe:
1. Send a single GraphQL request with 5 aliased login mutations:
   mutation {
     a1: login(email:"user@test.com", password:"pass1") { token }
     a2: login(email:"user@test.com", password:"pass2") { token }
     a3: login(email:"user@test.com", password:"pass3") { token }
     a4: login(email:"user@test.com", password:"pass4") { token }
     a5: login(email:"user@test.com", password:"pass5") { token }
   }
2. If all 5 operations return responses (tokens or errors), the batching bypass is confirmed.
3. Check if the rate limiter increments by 1 (the HTTP request) or by 5 (the operations).

INDICATOR: the server processes all 5 aliased operations and returns individual responses for each, and the rate limiter only counts 1 request.

Chainability: rate-limit bypass → credential stuffing → account takeover → IDOR → data exfiltration.
